<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bronze of Ascoli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">89 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> of Ascoli is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription on a bronze tablet recording that in 89 BCE the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-pompeius-strabo">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</a> granted Roman citizenship to a squadron of Iberian horsemen, the Turma Salluitana, for their bravery at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. Most of the riders came from towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salduie">Salduie</a>. Found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1908, it is a key source for Iberian personal names.</p></div>
